2. Axiomatic Derivation of the Decision‑Centric Objective

We ground the objective in normative decision theory. Consider an agent facing a finite set of possible future decision problems \mathcal{D}, each yielding an outcome evaluated by the agent. Before facing a decision, the agent may form an internal representation R of its observations, incurring a computational cost. The pair (R, \delta), where \delta is a policy mapping representations to decisions, is what the agent ultimately chooses.

Definition 1 (Representation–Decision Pair). Let \mathcal{R} be a set of possible representations (e.g., measurable functions of the observation history). Let \Delta(\mathcal{A}) be the set of randomized decision rules mapping a representation to a distribution over actions A\in\mathcal{A}. A pair (R, \delta)\in\mathcal{R}\times\Delta(\mathcal{A}) is a representation–decision pair.

The agent has a preference relation \succsim over such pairs. We impose six axioms:

A1 (Weak Order): \succsim is complete and transitive.
A2 (Continuity): For any (R,\delta), the sets \{(R',\delta') : (R',\delta')\succsim (R,\delta)\} and its complement are closed in a suitable topology.
A3 (Independence): For any (R_1,\delta_1), (R_2,\delta_2), (R_3,\delta_3) and \alpha\in(0,1), (R_1,\delta_1)\succsim(R_2,\delta_2) implies \alpha(R_1,\delta_1)+(1-\alpha)(R_3,\delta_3)\succsim\alpha(R_2,\delta_2)+(1-\alpha)(R_3,\delta_3). The mixture is interpreted as a probabilistic mixture of the pairs.
A4 (Monotonicity in Decisions): For a fixed R, the preference over \delta respects first‑order stochastic dominance of the induced distribution over decision outcomes.
A5 (Cost Separability): There exists a function C:\mathcal{R}\to\mathbb{R}_+ such that for any R_1,R_2 and any fixed \delta, (R_1,\delta)\succsim(R_2,\delta) iff C(R_1)\leq C(R_2). Furthermore, preferences over composite pairs are separable in the sense that \succsim can be represented as an additive utility U(R,\delta) = f(\delta) + g(R), with g strictly decreasing in C(R). This separability condition corresponds to the Debreu–Gorman additive representation over the mixture space of representations and decision rules; it is strictly weaker than requiring full vNM linearity for the cost term itself.
A6 (Resource Monotonicity): Increasing computational cost while holding the decision policy fixed makes the agent strictly worse off.

Remark. The cost function C(R) must be compatible with the mixture‑linearity of the representation space. Measures such as expected mutual information, expected FLOPs, or entropy satisfy this. Non‑linear costs like Kolmogorov complexity K(R) are not mixture‑linear and fall outside the direct axiomatic basis; when we later consider K(R), we interpret the objective via the Minimum Description Length principle, acknowledging the departure from the vNM framework.

Theorem 1 (Representation Theorem for Decision‑Centric Perception).
If \succsim satisfies A1–A6, then there exists a bounded real‑valued function V on decision outcomes and a constant \lambda>0 such that for all (R,\delta),

(R,\delta) \succsim (R',\delta') \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R,\delta)] - \lambda C(R) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R',\delta')] - \lambda C(R').

Moreover, when the agent optimizes the decision policy \delta for a given R, we obtain the value function

\mathcal{V}(R) = \max_{\delta} \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R,\delta)],

and the overall problem reduces to

R^* = \arg\max_{R\in\mathcal{R}} \left[ \mathcal{V}(R) - \lambda C(R) \right].

Proof sketch. By A1–A3 and the mixture‑space structure, the von Neumann–Morgenstern theorem yields a linear utility functional \mathcal{U} over pairs. Axiom A4 ensures that, conditional on R, the decision utility depends only on the outcome distribution. Axiom A5 invokes the additive separability (Debreu, 1960) to decompose the utility into a decision component and a cost component monotone in C. The trade‑off parameter \lambda emerges as the marginal rate of substitution. Uniqueness holds up to positive affine transformations and a scaling of C. ∎

Thus, the central equation is not an ad hoc postulate but a necessary consequence of rational preferences under resource limitations. The additive form mirrors the generalized information bottleneck objective recently formalized by Kamatsuka & Yoshida (2026), who prove that \mathcal{H}-mutual information equals the expected value of sample information and derive alternating algorithms for compression–utility trade‑offs. Our axiomatization provides a decision‑theoretic justification for their objective, grounding it in rational choice.

