3. Decision‑Centric Rate‑Distortion Theory

We now embed the objective within a precise probabilistic model of observation, state, and decision.

3.1 Formal Setting

Let the world be modeled as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP) with state space \mathcal{S}, observation space \mathcal{O}, action space \mathcal{A}, transition kernel P(s'|s,a), observation kernel P(o|s), and reward function r(s,a). We consider a batch of future decision tasks \mathcal{D} drawn from a distribution over POMDPs sharing the same state and observation spaces but possibly differing reward functions.

The agent first observes a history X (e.g., a sequence of observations) and constructs a stochastic representation R via an encoder p(r|x). The cost C(R) is measured as the mutual information I(X;R) between the raw data and the representation—a natural measure of the information extracted and stored.

After forming R, the agent faces a task‑specific decision process. Given the representation, it executes a policy \pi(a|r), yielding an expected return V^\pi(r). For an optimal task‑specific policy, we define the decision value

\mathcal{V}(R) = \mathbb{E}_{\text{task}}\!\left[ \max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t r_t \;\big|\; R, \pi \right] \right].

3.2 The Optimization Problem

The decision‑centric objective becomes

\max_{p(r|x)} \; \mathcal{V}(p(r|x)) - \lambda I(X;R), \tag{1}

where the maximization is over all stochastic encoders p(r|x) with a fixed representation alphabet \mathcal{R} (which may be continuous). We assume the optimal task policies are known or simultaneously optimized, so \mathcal{V} is a function of the encoder.

3.3 Existence of an Optimal Representation

Let X take values in a standard Borel space, and \mathcal{R} be a compact metric space. The set \Gamma of all couplings \gamma on X\times\mathcal{R} with X-marginal equal to the data distribution \mu is compact in the topology of weak convergence (Prokhorov’s theorem). We identify encoders with such couplings. Mutual information I_\gamma(X;R) is lower semi-continuous on \Gamma when \mathcal{R} is compact and the marginal \mu is tight (Posner, 1975); no uniform integrability condition is required.

Theorem 2 (Existence).
Assume \mathcal{R} is compact, and the decision value functional \mathcal{V}:\Gamma\to\mathbb{R} is upper semi-continuous in the weak topology. Then there exists an optimal encoder p^*(r|x) achieving the supremum in (1). If the optimization is restricted to a parametric family (e.g., neural encoders) whose closure in \Gamma is compact, an optimum exists in that family’s closure.

Proof. The objective J(\gamma) = \mathcal{V}(\gamma) - \lambda I_\gamma is upper semi-continuous on the compact set \Gamma; hence a maximum is attained. For parametric families, the same argument applies to their closed image. ∎

3.4 The Decision‑Distortion Function and Convexity

Define the maximal decision value achievable at rate C as

V^*(C) = \sup_{p(r|x): I(X;R) \le C} \mathcal{V}(p).

We first establish the structural properties of V^*(C).

Theorem 3 (Concavity and Monotonicity of V^*(C)).
V^*(C) is non‑decreasing, concave, and satisfies V^*(0) = \mathcal{V}(\text{no information}) and \lim_{C\to\infty} V^*(C) = \mathcal{V}_{\text{full}}.

Proof. Monotonicity is trivial. For concavity, consider two encoders achieving (C_1, V_1) and (C_2, V_2). Construct a time‑shared mixture that, with probability \alpha, uses the first encoder, and with probability 1-\alpha, uses the second, and reveals the chosen index to the decision maker. The resulting mutual information is exactly \alpha I_1 + (1-\alpha)I_2 because the index is known. The decision value becomes \alpha V_1 + (1-\alpha)V_2, by the ability to condition the policy on the revealed index. Taking the supremum yields V^*(\alpha C_1+(1-\alpha)C_2) \ge \alpha V^*(C_1)+(1-\alpha)V^*(C_2). Concavity follows. ∎

Theorem 4 (Lagrange–Constraint Equivalence).
For any \lambda>0, the solution to the Lagrangian (1) also solves the constrained problem \max_{p} \mathcal{V}(p) subject to I(X;R)\le C_\lambda for some C_\lambda non‑increasing in \lambda. Conversely, any constrained optimum with a tight constraint is achievable via a suitable Lagrange multiplier \lambda.

Proof. The set of achievable pairs (I,\mathcal{V}) is convex (by the time‑sharing argument). The Lagrangian maximizer \gamma_\lambda yields a point on the upper boundary. Standard supporting hyperplane arguments for concave V^*(C) provide the equivalence. ∎

This “decision‑distortion” curve replaces the classic rate‑distortion function and characterizes the fundamental trade‑off of perception.

3.5 Information Bottleneck as a Special Case

If the decision task consists solely of predicting a target variable Y with a logarithmic loss, then \mathcal{V}(R) reduces to the mutual information I(R;Y) (up to constants). The objective (1) becomes

\max_{p(r|x)} I(R;Y) - \lambda I(X;R),

which is exactly Tishby’s Information Bottleneck (IB) Lagrangian. Thus, the IB principle emerges as a special case of decision‑centric perception when the only “decision” is prediction with log‑loss.

