4. Sufficient Statistics, Blackwell Order, and Decision Equivalence

We now address the question: When are two representations equivalent with respect to decision value?

4.1 Decision Value as a Function of the Posterior

Given an encoder p(r|x), the representation provides a distribution over the unobserved state s. The decision value for a specific task T can be written as

V_T(R) = \mathbb{E}_{x}\!\left[ \max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}_{s\sim p(s|x)} [ Q_T^*(s, \pi(r)) ] \right],

where Q_T^* is the optimal state‑action value function. It is clear that V_T depends on x only through the posterior p(s|x); hence, the representation R is useful only to the extent that it captures this posterior.

Definition 2 (Decision Sufficiency).
A representation R (induced by p(r|x)) is decision‑sufficient for a task set \mathcal{T} if, for almost all x, the distribution of the optimal value function V_T^* given r is identical to that given x for every T\in\mathcal{T}.

4.2 Minimal Sufficient Representation

Assume the family of observation distributions \{p(x|s)\} is dominated by a common \sigma-finite measure. Let \mathbf{V}^* = \{V_T^*(s)\}_{T\in\mathcal{T}}.

Theorem 5 (Minimal Decision‑Sufficient Statistic).
Among all decision‑sufficient encoders, the one minimizing I(X;R) is a minimal sufficient statistic of X for \mathbf{V}^*. This encoder is unique up to almost‑sure bijections and can be constructed by taking R as the posterior p(\mathbf{V}^*\mid X).

Proof. The dominatedness ensures existence of a minimal sufficient statistic for \mathbf{V}^* (Bahadur, 1954). Decision sufficiency of R means R is sufficient for \mathbf{V}^*; the minimal sufficient statistic discards all irrelevant variability and hence minimizes I(X;R). ∎

4.3 Blackwell Order and Decision Equivalence

The preceding uniqueness statement is “up to bijection”, but a deeper structural insight comes from Blackwell’s theorem on comparison of experiments. A representation p(r|x) defines a statistical experiment; the decision value depends on the experiment only through its information structure.

Theorem 6 (Blackwell Characterization of Optimal Representations).
For any two encoders p_1(r|x) and p_2(r|x), the following are equivalent:

(i) p_1 is Blackwell sufficient for p_2, i.e., p_2 can be obtained as a garbling (randomized post‑processing) of p_1.
(ii) For every decision task with bounded utility, the optimal expected reward using p_1 is at least that using p_2.

Consequently, any two representations that achieve the maximal decision value for a given task class are Blackwell‑equivalent: they are garbles of each other. The optimal representation is unique only up to this Blackwell equivalence class. This formalizes and extends the decision bisimulation perspective.

Proof. This is a direct application of the Blackwell–Sherman–Stein theorem (see Paxton, 2026, for a modern survey). The task class \mathcal{T} indexes a family of state‑dependent utility functions; Blackwell’s theorem states that p_1 dominates p_2 in this sense exactly when p_2 is a garbling of p_1. ∎

4.4 Decision Bisimulation Pseudometric

Define the pseudometric between representations directly:

d(R_1, R_2) = \sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}} \left| \mathbb{E}\!\big[ V_T^*(R_1) \big] - \mathbb{E}\!\big[ V_T^*(R_2) \big] \right|.

Theorem 7 (Pseudometric Properties).
d is a pseudometric. d(R_1,R_2)=0 iff R_1 and R_2 are decision‑equivalent (same expected value for all tasks). If \mathcal{T} is rich enough, d becomes a metric on the quotient space of Blackwell equivalence classes.

Proof. Symmetry, non‑negativity, and triangle inequality are immediate. The zero condition corresponds to identical performance across all tasks, which by Blackwell’s theorem implies the representations are equivalent up to garbling. ∎

4.5 Geometry and Wasserstein Connection

If the optimal policy \pi^*(a|s) varies smoothly with s, the Fisher information metric of \pi^* induces a Riemannian structure. Representation embeddings that preserve decision‑relevant dissimilarities must pull back this metric. Moreover, for Lipschitz value functions, the decision pseudometric is bounded by the average Wasserstein distance of the induced state posteriors:

d(R_1,R_2) \le L \cdot \mathbb{E}_X\!\bigl[W_2(p(s|R_1), p(s|R_2))\bigr].

