5. Emergence of Common Perceptual Mechanisms

The universal objective (1) adapts the structure of the optimal representation to the underlying task distribution and to the precise form of the cost C(R). In this section, we rigorously distinguish between two cost types—mutual information I(X;R) and entropy/cardinality constraints—and show how different perceptual phenomena emerge.

5.1 The Cost Determines the Form: Rate vs. Entropy Constraints

When C(R) = I(X;R), the optimization (1) is a rate‑distortion problem with a decision‑based distortion measure. If, instead, we constrain the cardinality |\mathcal{R}|\le M or penalize the entropy H(R), we obtain a quantization or categorization problem. The distinction is crucial, as the classical results on optimal test channels differ fundamentally.

Theorem 8a (Mutual‑Information Cost — Gaussian Channels).
Consider a scalar observation X drawn from a Gaussian distribution and a quadratic decision value (e.g., LQG control). Under the cost C(R)=I(X;R), the optimal encoder p(r|x) is not a discrete quantizer. For Gaussian X\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_x^2) and a squared‑error distortion equivalent to the decision value, the optimal test channel achieving the rate–distortion bound is R = \alpha X + Z with Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,D), independent of X. This yields a linear policy a = K \mathbb{E}[s|R] in the control task. Continuous representations are optimal.

Proof. This is the classical Gaussian rate–distortion result (Cover & Thomas, 2006). The decision value is monotone in the squared error of the state estimate. The optimal channel is Gaussian, not discrete. ∎

Theorem 8b (Entropy or Cardinality Cost — Categorization).
If instead the cost is C(R)=H(R) (entropy) or a hard cardinality constraint |\mathcal{R}|\le M, and the decision value is a quadratic loss, the optimal encoder partitions the observation space into a finite number of intervals (Lloyd–Max quantization). The number of intervals grows as the cost coefficient decreases. Thus, discrete perceptual categories emerge under entropy‑limited, not rate‑limited, resources.

Proof. Under entropy or cardinality constraints, the problem becomes the classic entropy‑constrained quantization (Gersho & Gray, 1992). The optimal encoder is a deterministic partition, with reconstruction levels given by the conditional expectation within each cell. ∎

The distinction between Theorems 8a and 8b clarifies a subtle point often confused in the literature: discrete symbolic perception is optimal for memory‑bound agents, whereas continuous latent spaces are optimal when the cost is the information rate itself.

5.2 Reconstruction and Segmentation as Special Cases

Theorem 9 (Reconstruction Loss).
Suppose the set of future tasks consists of uniformly randomly querying the value of individual pixels (i.e., the “decision” is to predict each pixel’s intensity). With a log‑loss decision utility, \mathcal{V}(R) reduces to -\mathbb{E}_x[\text{MSE}(x,\hat{x}(R))] and the objective (1) is equivalent to training a variational autoencoder with a rate term \lambda I(X;R). Thus, reconstruction‑based vision is optimal only when the universe of future decisions is a uniform pixel‑prediction task.

Theorem 10 (Segmentation Loss).
When tasks are semantic segmentation with a cross‑entropy loss, the decision value becomes the negative cross‑entropy, and the objective aligns with the information bottleneck for dense labeling. Hence, segmentation is optimal for a task distribution concentrated on pixel‑wise classification.

5.3 Object‑Centric Representations

Assume the decision value decomposes additively over independent objects:

V(D\mid s) = \sum_{\text{objects } i} V_i(D_i\mid s_i),

where s_i are object‑specific states, and no inter‑object interactions exist in the future tasks.

Theorem 11 (Object‑Centric Factorization).
Under the additive value assumption, the optimal representation under a mutual information cost factorizes into independent object slots R = (R_1, R_2, \dots, R_K), with each R_i encoding only the i-th object’s properties. Any representation that entangles information across objects incurs a higher mutual information cost for the same decision value and is strictly suboptimal.

Proof. The additive value structure implies that the minimal sufficient statistic can be computed per object. Mutual information is subadditive: a factorized encoding achieves the same decision value with lower or equal total I(X;R). ∎

5.4 Emergence of Attention

Spatial attention corresponds to a representation that extracts only a subset of input locations.

Theorem 12 (Hard Attention as Knapsack Allocation).
If the cost C(R) penalizes the total number of features processed and the decision value depends only on a sparse set of image regions, then the optimal representation includes a selection mask (hard attention). The selected regions are those with the highest marginal decision information per unit compute, solving a knapsack problem:

\max_{\mathcal{M}} \sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} I(\text{region}_i; V^*) \quad\text{s.t. } |\mathcal{M}| \le B.

Proof. The decision value contribution of a region is measured by the mutual information between that region and the optimal value function. Under a budget on the number of regions, the greedy selection of highest I(\text{region}_i; V^*) is optimal for the linear‑knapsack proxy. ∎

