6. Temporal Extension: Active Sequential Perception

In real vision systems, perception is not a one‑shot computation. The agent can actively select sensory inputs and sequentially refine its representation. We model this as a meta‑POMDP.

6.1 Dynamic Programming and Optimal Stopping

Let R_t be the representation at time t, updated using new observations and computation actions u_t. The cost is additive: C_{\text{total}} = \sum_t \gamma^t C(R_t, u_t). The optimal value function satisfies

Q(R) = \max\!\Big\{
\underbrace{\max_a \big[ r + \gamma \mathbb{E}[Q(R')] \big]}_{\text{act with current } R},\;
\underbrace{\max_u \big[ -C(R,u) + \mathbb{E}[Q(R'')] \big]}_{\text{continue perception}}
\Big\}.

Theorem 13 (Optimal Stopping).
If the expected improvement \mathbb{E}[\Delta Q] from one additional perception step is monotonically decreasing in the amount of information already acquired, there exists a threshold \tau such that it is optimal to stop perceiving and act when \max_u \mathbb{E}[\Delta Q] \le C_{\text{expected}}. This formalizes satisficing: the agent continues only as long as marginal decision value exceeds marginal cost.

Proof. Under the monotone decreasing property, the problem falls into the monotone optimal stopping class (Shiryaev, 1978), and the stopping set is a threshold. ∎

6.2 Connection to Active Inference

Active inference prescribes that agents minimize expected free energy G. In our framework, choose \mathcal{V}(R) = \mathbb{E}_{q(s|R)}[\log p(o|R)] and cost C(R) = D_{\text{KL}}(q(s|R)\|p(s)), with \lambda=1. Then the sequential problem becomes

\min_{p(r|x), \pi} \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_t \big( G_t + \lambda I(X_t;R_t) \big) \right].

Theorem 14 (Active Inference as a Special Bounded‑Rational Case).
When the decision value is the negative expected free energy and the cost is exactly the KL divergence from prior to posterior (a specific information measure), the decision‑centric dynamic program coincides with active inference. For general \lambda and alternative cost functions, our framework provides a bounded‑rational generalization of active inference, where perception–action loops are driven by the trade‑off between extrinsic value and computational resource expenditure.

Proof. Substituting the special choices into the dynamic programming equation recovers the variational free energy minimization (Friston et al., 2017). Our formulation with adjustable \lambda generalizes it to settings where the agent has finite information‑processing capacity, much like the bounded‑rational control laws derived by Genewein et al. (2015). ∎

