7. Tractability and Optimization

7.1 Variational Lower Bound

Direct maximization of \mathcal{V}(R) is intractable. We introduce a critic Q_\phi(r,a) approximating the optimal action‑value function.

Theorem 15 (Variational Decision Bound).
For any critic Q_\phi,

\mathcal{V}(R) \ge \mathbb{E}_{x,r\sim p(r|x)}\!\left[ \max_a Q_\phi(r,a) \right] - \text{ErrorTerm},

with equality when Q_\phi is exact. This yields the tractable surrogate objective

\max_{p_\theta(r|x), Q_\phi} \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \max_a Q_\phi(r,a) \right] - \lambda I(X;R) - \mathcal{L}_{\text{critic}},

where \mathcal{L}_{\text{critic}} is a standard TD error.

7.2 Gradient Estimation

Theorem 16 (Representation Gradient).
Under a softmax decision policy \pi_\psi(a|r), the gradient of \mathcal{V} w.r.t. encoder parameters \theta is

\nabla_\theta \mathcal{V} = \mathbb{E}_{x,r\sim p_\theta(\cdot|x), a\sim\pi_\psi}\!\left[ \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(r|x) \cdot \hat{Q}(r,a) \right],

allowing unbiased Monte Carlo estimation.

7.3 PAC‑Bayes Sample Complexity

A natural question is sample efficiency. Using a PAC‑Bayes bound with a prior P over encoder parameters \theta, we obtain:

Theorem 17 (PAC‑Bayes Bound for Decision‑Centric Encoders).
Let \rho be a data‑dependent posterior over parameters, and P a fixed prior. With probability at least 1-\delta over the sampling of a dataset of size N,

\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\mathcal{V}(\theta)] \ge \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\widehat{\mathcal{V}}(\theta)] - \sqrt{\frac{\text{KL}(\rho\|P) + \log\frac{N}{\delta}}{2N}}.

The information cost I(X;R) can be linked to the KL term via an information bottleneck bound: I(X;R) \le \mathbb{E}_{x}[\text{KL}(p_\theta(r|x)\|p(r))], and choosing a parameter prior that induces a desired marginal p(r) connects the regularizer to the generalization guarantee.

Proof. Standard PAC‑Bayes theorem for bounded loss. The bound on mutual information follows from the variational identity I(X;R) = \min_{q(r)} \mathbb{E}_x[\text{KL}(p(r|x)\|q(r))] (Alemi et al., 2016). ∎

7.4 Implicit Bias of Gradient Descent

When optimizing the variational objective with SGD, the dynamics exhibit an implicit bias toward minimal sufficient statistics. The mutual information regularizer explicitly penalizes redundant information, and in the overparameterized regime, gradient descent converges to solutions that discard all variability not linearly separable with respect to the decision value. While a full convergence proof remains an open problem, the phenomenology is consistent with the informational bottleneck principle.

7.5 Convergence for the Gaussian IB Surrogate

Theorem 18 (Convergence of Gaussian IB).
The iterative Blahut–Arimoto algorithm for the Gaussian information bottleneck converges to the global optimum of \max I(R;Y) - \lambda I(X;R) for jointly Gaussian (X,Y). This provides a provably convergent building block for more complex decision objectives.

