A. Proofs for Section 2 (Axiomatic Derivation)

A.1 Mixture Space and Preliminaries

The set \mathcal{R}\times\Delta(\mathcal{A}) is endowed with a mixture operation: for \alpha\in[0,1],

\alpha(R_1,\delta_1) + (1-\alpha)(R_2,\delta_2)

is the pair obtained by flipping an \alpha-biased coin and then executing the corresponding (representation, decision rule) pair. The coin’s outcome is observed, so the decision rule can condition on it. Formally, this constructs a new representation in an augmented space containing the coin flip, and the mixture is well‑defined. Under the closure properties assumed, this mixture satisfies the algebraic axioms of a mixture space (Herstein & Milnor, 1953).

A.2 Proof of Theorem 1 (Representation Theorem)

Theorem 1. Suppose \succsim satisfies Axioms A1–A6. Then there exists a bounded function V:\mathcal{D}\to\mathbb{R}, a cost functional C:\mathcal{R}\to\mathbb{R}_+, and a constant \lambda>0 such that

(R,\delta) \succsim (R',\delta') \iff \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R,\delta)] - \lambda C(R) \ge \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R',\delta')] - \lambda C(R').

The function C and the scalar \lambda are unique up to a common scaling; V is unique up to a positive affine transformation.

Proof.
Step 1: Linear utility on the mixture space. A1 (weak order), A2 (continuity), and A3 (independence) are precisely the von Neumann–Morgenstern axioms on a mixture space. By the Mixture‑Space Theorem (Herstein & Milnor, 1953; Fishburn, 1970), there exists a real‑valued linear functional U that represents \succsim:

U(\alpha(R_1,\delta_1)+(1-\alpha)(R_2,\delta_2)) = \alpha U(R_1,\delta_1)+(1-\alpha)U(R_2,\delta_2).

U is unique up to a positive affine transformation.

Step 2: Additive separability. Fix an arbitrary baseline pair (R_0,\delta_0). Axiom A5 (Cost Separability) asserts the existence of a function C such that for any fixed \delta, (R_1,\delta)\succsim(R_2,\delta) iff C(R_1)\le C(R_2). Moreover, A5 states that preferences are separable in the sense of the Debreu–Gorman additive representation: the overall utility can be written as U(R,\delta) = f(\delta) + g(R), with g(R) monotone decreasing in C(R). This additive decomposition follows because the marginal preference over representations (for any fixed \delta) is independent of the particular \delta—a strong separability condition. One can prove existence of the additive form by defining

g(R) := U(R,\delta_0) - U(R_0,\delta_0),

f(\delta) := U(R_0,\delta) - U(R_0,\delta_0),

and verifying that additivity holds for all mixtures using linearity and the independence of marginal preferences. Since g(R) is strictly decreasing in C(R) (by A6), there exists \lambda>0 and an affine transformation such that g(R) = -\lambda C(R).

Step 3: Decision utility as expected von Neumann–Morgenstern utility. Axiom A4 requires that for a fixed R, the preference over \delta respects first‑order stochastic dominance of the induced outcome distributions. By standard expected utility theory, this implies that the component f(\delta) can be expressed as the expectation of a cardinal utility function V over outcomes:

f(\delta) = \mathbb{E}_{D\sim P(\cdot\mid R,\delta)}[V(D)].

The function V inherits boundedness from the continuity axiom. Because R only affects the distribution of outcomes through the decision rule \delta and the posterior over states, the full utility becomes

U(R,\delta) = \mathbb{E}[V(D\mid R,\delta)] - \lambda C(R).

The uniqueness claims follow from standard vNM uniqueness and the additive decomposition. ∎

