B. Proofs for Section 3 (Decision‑Centric Rate‑Distortion)

B.1 Preliminaries on Couplings and Topology

Let X be a standard Borel space with probability measure \mu. The representation alphabet \mathcal{R} is a compact metric space. A stochastic encoder is identified with a coupling \gamma\in\Gamma, the set of probability measures on X\times\mathcal{R} having X-marginal \mu. Endow \Gamma with the topology of weak convergence. Because \mu is a single tight measure and \mathcal{R} is compact, \Gamma is compact by Prokhorov’s theorem (Billingsley, 1999, Theorem 5.1). The mutual information I_\gamma(X;R) for a coupling is defined in the usual way, setting it to +\infty if the Radon–Nikodym derivative does not exist. We will need the following standard fact:

Lemma B.1 (Lower Semicontinuity of Mutual Information).
If \mathcal{R} is compact and \mu is tight, then I_\gamma(X;R) is lower semicontinuous on \Gamma with respect to the weak topology.

Proof. Under the stated conditions, \Gamma is a compact subset of probability measures on a Polish space. The mutual information can be expressed as

I_\gamma = \sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}} \Bigl( \mathbb{E}_\gamma[f(X,R)] - \log \mathbb{E}_{\mu\otimes\nu_\gamma}[e^{f(X,R)}] \Bigr)

for a suitable class of bounded continuous functions, which is the supremum of continuous functionals and hence lower semicontinuous (Posner, 1975; see also Ghourchian et al., 2021). No uniform integrability is required in this setting. ∎

The decision value \mathcal{V}:\Gamma\to\mathbb{R} is assumed upper semicontinuous. This holds, for instance, when the rewards are bounded and the optimal policy is continuous in the representation.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 2 (Existence)

Theorem 2. Under the above compactness and semicontinuity assumptions, there exists an optimal encoder p^*(r|x) attaining \sup_{\gamma\in\Gamma} [\mathcal{V}(\gamma) - \lambda I_\gamma].

Proof. The objective J(\gamma) = \mathcal{V}(\gamma) - \lambda I_\gamma is the difference of an upper semicontinuous function and a lower semicontinuous function, thus it is upper semicontinuous. The domain \Gamma is compact. An upper semicontinuous function on a compact set attains its maximum, yielding the desired existence. ∎

B.3 Proof of Theorem 3 (Concavity and Monotonicity of V^*(C))

Define V^*(C) = \sup \{\mathcal{V}(\gamma) : I_\gamma \le C\}.

Theorem 3. V^*(C) is non‑decreasing, concave, and satisfies V^*(0) = \mathcal{V}(\text{no information}) and \lim_{C\to\infty}V^*(C) = \mathcal{V}_{\text{full}}.

Proof. Monotonicity is trivial because a larger C enlarges the feasible set. To prove concavity, take any two rates C_1, C_2 and \alpha\in[0,1]. For any \varepsilon>0, choose encoders \gamma_1,\gamma_2 feasible for C_1,C_2 with \mathcal{V}(\gamma_i) \ge V^*(C_i) - \varepsilon. Construct a time‑shared encoder: with probability \alpha use \gamma_1, with probability 1-\alpha use \gamma_2, and append a single bit b indicating which branch was taken to the representation. The decision rule can condition on b. Then the resulting mutual information is

I = \alpha I_{\gamma_1} + (1-\alpha) I_{\gamma_2} \le \alpha C_1 + (1-\alpha) C_2,

since conditioning on b removes any additional correlation. The decision value becomes \mathcal{V} = \alpha \mathcal{V}(\gamma_1) + (1-\alpha)\mathcal{V}(\gamma_2) because the optimal policy can adapt to b. Thus

V^*(\alpha C_1+(1-\alpha)C_2) \ge \alpha V^*(C_1) + (1-\alpha) V^*(C_2) - \varepsilon.

Letting \varepsilon\to0 establishes concavity. The boundary values are immediate from the definitions. ∎

B.4 Proof of Theorem 4 (Lagrange–Constraint Equivalence)

Theorem 4. For every \lambda>0, the Lagrangian maximizer solves a constrained problem with capacity C_\lambda. Conversely, any tight constrained optimum corresponds to some \lambda.

Proof. The set \mathcal{A} = \{(I,\mathcal{V})\} of achievable pairs is convex, as shown by the time‑sharing argument with revealed coin flip. The Lagrangian \mathcal{V} - \lambda I is a linear functional. Its maximizer \gamma_\lambda lies on the upper boundary of \mathcal{A}. By the Supporting Hyperplane Theorem, there exists a slope \lambda such that the line with slope \lambda supports \mathcal{A} at (I_{\gamma_\lambda}, \mathcal{V}(\gamma_\lambda)). For any \gamma with I\le I_{\gamma_\lambda}, we have \mathcal{V}(\gamma) - \lambda I \le \mathcal{V}(\gamma_\lambda) - \lambda I_{\gamma_\lambda}. If \mathcal{V}(\gamma) > \mathcal{V}(\gamma_\lambda), the inequality would be violated (since I \le I_{\gamma_\lambda}), so \mathcal{V}(\gamma) \le \mathcal{V}(\gamma_\lambda). Hence \gamma_\lambda maximizes \mathcal{V} subject to the capacity C_\lambda = I_{\gamma_\lambda}. Conversely, given a tight constrained optimum, the point (C, V^*) lies on the concave boundary; a supporting line with slope \lambda exists, proving the equivalence. ∎

