C. Proofs for Section 4 (Sufficiency, Blackwell Order, and Equivalence)

C.1 Proof of Theorem 5 (Minimal Decision‑Sufficient Statistic)

Theorem 5. Under the assumption that the family \{P(\cdot|s)\} is dominated, there exists a minimal sufficient statistic for \mathbf{V}^*. An encoder is decision‑sufficient iff it is a sufficient statistic for \mathbf{V}^*. Among all such, the one minimizing I(X;R) is unique up to bijection and equals the posterior p(\mathbf{V}^*\mid X).

Proof. Dominatedness ensures that the family of distributions of X indexed by the value vector \mathbf{V}^* is dominated. By the factorization theorem (Bahadur, 1954), the posterior p(\mathbf{V}^*\mid X) is a minimal sufficient statistic for \mathbf{V}^*. A representation R is decision‑sufficient exactly when the conditional distribution of \mathbf{V}^* given R equals that given X, i.e., R is a sufficient statistic for \mathbf{V}^*. Since mutual information satisfies the data‑processing inequality, any additional randomness beyond the minimal sufficient statistic can only increase I(X;R). Hence the minimal sufficient statistic uniquely minimizes I(X;R) among all decision‑sufficient encoders. ∎

C.2 Blackwell’s Theorem and Proof of Theorem 6

Definition C.1 (Blackwell Sufficiency). Encoder \gamma_1 is Blackwell sufficient for \gamma_2 if there exists a Markov kernel h(r_2|r_1) such that the conditional distribution of R_2 given X can be obtained by composing \gamma_1 and h:

p_2(r_2|x) = \int p_1(r_1|x) h(r_2|r_1) dr_1.

Theorem 6 (Blackwell Characterization). For two encoders, the following are equivalent:

1. \gamma_1 is Blackwell sufficient for \gamma_2.
2. For every decision problem with state space \mathcal{S}, action set \mathcal{A}, and bounded utility u(s,a), the maximal expected utility using \gamma_1 is at least that using \gamma_2.

Consequently, any two optimal encoders for the task class are Blackwell‑equivalent.

Proof. This is a reformulation of Blackwell’s Theorem on comparison of experiments (Blackwell, 1953; see Paxton, 2026 for a modern exposition). In our setting, the observation X plays the role of the statistical experiment. The task class \mathcal{T} induces a family of state‑dependent utility functions U(s,a). The decision maker observes R (a garbled version of X) and chooses an action. Blackwell’s result states precisely that the ranking by maximal expected utility for all such decision problems corresponds to the garbling order. Therefore, if two encoders achieve the same maximal decision value for all tasks, each must be a garbling of the other, making them equivalent in the Blackwell order. ∎

C.3 Proof of Theorem 7 (Pseudometric Properties)

Theorem 7. d(R_1,R_2) = \sup_{T} |\mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_1)] - \mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_2)]| is a pseudometric, with d=0 iff R_1,R_2 are Blackwell‑equivalent.

Proof. Non‑negativity, symmetry, and the triangle inequality follow from the properties of absolute value and supremum. If d(R_1,R_2)=0, then \mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_1)] = \mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_2)] for all T. By Theorem 6, this means the two encoders are Blackwell‑equivalent (each is a garbling of the other), because the condition of equal performance for all possible utility functions implies mutual garbling. The class of tasks \mathcal{T} must be rich enough to differentiate states; if not, the distance is only a pseudometric on the quotient space. ∎

C.4 Wasserstein Bound (Proposition)

Proposition C.2. Suppose all value functions V_T^* are L-Lipschitz with respect to a base metric on \mathcal{S}. Then

d(R_1,R_2) \le L \cdot \mathbb{E}_X\!\bigl[W_2(p(s|R_1), p(s|R_2))\bigr].

Proof. Fix a task T. Using the dual formulation of the 1‑Wasserstein distance,

|\mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_1)] - \mathbb{E}[V_T^*(R_2)]| \le \mathbb{E}_X\!\bigl[| \mathbb{E}_{s|R_1}[V_T^*(s)] - \mathbb{E}_{s|R_2}[V_T^*(s)] |\bigr] \le L \cdot \mathbb{E}_X[W_1(p_1,p_2)].

Since W_1 \le W_2, the result follows. ∎

