D. Proofs for Section 5 (Emergence of Perceptual Mechanisms)

D.1 Proof of Theorem 8a (Gaussian Channel Under Mutual Information Cost)

Theorem 8a. For X\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_x^2) and a squared‑error distortion equivalent to decision value, the optimal test channel minimizing I(X;R) subject to an MSE constraint is R = \alpha X + Z with Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,D) independent, where \alpha = 1 - D/\sigma_x^2. Consequently, the optimal policy in an LQG control problem with this representation is linear.

Proof. The decision value \mathcal{V}(R) is monotone decreasing in \mathbb{E}[(s-\hat{s}(R))^2] for the state estimate. Under Gaussian noise, the mutual information is minimized for a given distortion by a joint Gaussian distribution on (X,R) (Cover & Thomas, 2006, §10.3.2). The optimal conditional distribution p(r|x) is \mathcal{N}(r; \alpha x, (1-\alpha^2)\sigma_x^2 D/(\sigma_x^2-D)). For the LQG setting, this yields a linear estimator for the state and hence a linear optimal control policy. ∎

D.2 Proof of Theorem 8b (Categorization Under Entropy/Cardinality Cost)

Theorem 8b. If the cost is H(R) or a cardinality bound |\mathcal{R}|\le M, and the decision value is a squared‑error loss, then the optimal encoder partitions the real line into M intervals, with quantization levels given by the conditional expectation in each cell (Lloyd–Max quantizer). The number of intervals grows as the cost multiplier decreases.

Proof. With C(R)=H(R), the problem becomes \min \mathbb{E}[d(X,\hat{X})] + \lambda H(R) — the classic entropy‑constrained quantization problem. The optimal reconstruction levels and partition satisfy the Lloyd–Max conditions: each cell is a Voronoi region, and each reconstruction level is the centroid of its cell. When the distribution is continuous, the optimal quantizer is a deterministic partition into intervals. The number of intervals is controlled by \lambda; as \lambda\to0, the rate increases. A cardinality constraint |\mathcal{R}|\le M yields the same structure with a hard bound. See Gersho & Gray (1992) for a full treatment. ∎

D.3 Proof of Theorem 9 (Reconstruction Loss)

Theorem 9. Under uniform pixel‑wise query tasks with log‑loss, \mathcal{V}(R) = -\mathbb{E}_X[\mathrm{MSE}(X,\hat{X}(R))] up to constants, and the objective (1) coincides with the \beta-VAE loss.

Proof. The decision is to output a distribution q(\hat{x}_i|R) for each pixel. The log‑loss utility is \log q(x_i). Taking expectation over uniformly queried pixels yields -\mathbb{E}_X[-\log q(X|R)]. The optimal q for a fixed R minimizes cross‑entropy, which is proportional to \log p(X|R) up to an additive constant (entropy of X). For Gaussian observation noise, this becomes MSE. Adding the rate term \lambda I(X;R) gives the \beta-VAE objective \mathbb{E}_{q(R|X)}[\log p(X|R)] - \lambda D_{KL}(q(R|X)\|p(R)). ∎

D.4 Proof of Theorem 10 (Segmentation Loss)

Analogous to Theorem 9, replacing pixel intensity prediction with per‑pixel class label prediction under cross‑entropy loss.

D.5 Proof of Theorem 11 (Object‑Centric Factorization)

Theorem 11. Assume V(D|s) = \sum_i V_i(D_i|s_i) with no inter‑object interactions. Then the optimal encoder factorizes into independent object slots R=(R_1,\dots,R_K), each encoding only object i.

Proof. The minimal sufficient statistic for the vector \mathbf{V}^* = (V_1^*,\dots,V_K^*) under the additive structure is the collection of individual posteriors p(V_i^*|X). Because the objects are independent in the decision value, the mutual information decomposes as I(X;R_1,\dots,R_K) \ge \sum_i I(X;R_i) when the R_i are conditionally independent given X. The additive structure allows us to design an encoder that outputs a tuple of independent representations, one per object. Any encoder that entangles information across objects cannot have lower mutual information for the same decision value (by the data‑processing inequality applied per component). Thus the factorized representation is optimal. ∎

D.6 Proof of Theorem 12 (Hard Attention as Knapsack)

Theorem 12. With a cost proportional to the number of selected image regions, the optimal attention mask selects those regions with highest I(\text{region}_i; V^*).

Proof. The decision value depends on the selected regions only through the posterior of the value function. The cost is the count of selected regions. The problem is a knapsack: maximize total decision information \sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} I_i subject to |\mathcal{M}|\le B. Since the items are independent (or can be approximated as such), the greedy selection by marginal information content is optimal. ∎

