E. Proofs for Section 6 (Sequential Perception)

E.1 Proof of Theorem 13 (Optimal Stopping)

Theorem 13. Assume the expected improvement \Delta_k = \mathbb{E}[Q(R_{k+1}) - Q(R_k)] is non‑increasing in the amount of information acquired. Then the optimal policy stops when \max_u \mathbb{E}[\Delta Q] \le C_{\text{expected}}.

Proof. Under the monotone decreasing condition, the optimal stopping problem belongs to the monotone case (Shiryaev, 1978, Ch. 3). The continuation region is of the form \{\Delta \ge \tau\} for some threshold \tau. The threshold is exactly the expected one‑step cost of continuing, leading to the stated condition. ∎

E.2 Proof of Theorem 14 (Active Inference as Special Bounded‑Rational Case)

Theorem 14. With the specific choices \mathcal{V}(R) = \mathbb{E}_{q(s|R)}[\log p(o|R)] and C(R)=D_{KL}(q(s|R)\|p(s)), \lambda=1, the sequential decision‑centric objective reduces to active inference’s free energy minimization. For general \lambda and costs, it generalizes to a bounded‑rational variant.

Proof. The expected free energy in active inference is G = -\mathbb{E}_q[\log p(o|s)] + D_{KL}(q(s)\|p(s)) - \mathbb{E}_q[\log p(a|s)]. With the selected utility (log‑evidence of observations) and cost (KL divergence from prior to posterior), the Lagrangian becomes \mathbb{E}[\log p(o|R)] - D_{KL}(q\|p). Substituting into the dynamic programming equation yields the Bellman recursion for expected free energy with an additional information penalty. This matches the active inference objective when \lambda=1 and the utility is exactly the expected log‑likelihood. For \lambda\neq1, we obtain a bounded‑rational generalization where the agent can trade off extrinsic value against internal model complexity, akin to the control framework of Genewein et al. (2015). ∎

