F. Proofs for Section 7 (Tractability)

F.1 Proof of Theorem 15 (Variational Lower Bound)

Theorem 15. For any critic Q_\phi,

\mathcal{V}(R) \ge \mathbb{E}_{x,r\sim p(r|x)}\!\bigl[\max_a Q_\phi(r,a)\bigr] - \mathbb{E}\bigl[| \max_a Q^*(r,a) - \max_a Q_\phi(r,a) |\bigr],

where Q^* is the true optimal action‑value function.

Proof. By definition, \mathcal{V}(R) = \mathbb{E}[\max_a Q^*(r,a)]. For any fixed Q_\phi, the policy that chooses a^* = \arg\max Q_\phi yields expected return at most \mathbb{E}[\max_a Q_\phi] minus the approximation error. More precisely,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[\max_a Q^*\bigr] \ge \mathbb{E}\bigl[\max_a Q_\phi\bigr] - \mathbb{E}\bigl[|\max_a Q^* - \max_a Q_\phi|\bigr].

Dropping the error term yields a lower bound. ∎

F.2 Proof of Theorem 16 (Representation Gradient)

Theorem 16. Under a softmax policy \pi_\psi(a|r) \propto \exp(\tau Q_\phi(r,a)),

\nabla_\theta \mathcal{V} = \mathbb{E}_{x,r\sim p_\theta(\cdot|x), a\sim\pi_\psi}\!\bigl[ \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(r|x) \cdot \hat{Q}(r,a) \bigr].

Proof. The expected return can be written as \mathbb{E}_{p_\theta(r|x)\pi_\psi(a|r)}[Q^*(r,a)]. Differentiating w.r.t. \theta and using the log‑derivative trick gives the score‑function estimator. The gradient of \log\pi_\psi w.r.t. \theta is zero because the policy parameters are fixed (or can be treated as fixed for the encoder gradient). Unbiasedness follows from the standard REINFORCE derivation. ∎

F.3 Proof of Theorem 17 (PAC‑Bayes Bound)

Theorem 17. Let \rho be a distribution over encoder parameters \theta and P a prior. With probability 1-\delta over N samples,

\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\mathcal{V}(\theta)] \ge \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\widehat{\mathcal{V}}(\theta)] - \sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{KL}(\rho\|P) + \log(N/\delta)}{2N}}.

Moreover, I(X;R) \le \mathbb{E}_x[\mathrm{KL}(p_\theta(r|x)\|q(r))] for any marginal q, linking the regularizer to the bound.

Proof. The loss \ell(\theta) = -\mathcal{V}(\theta) is bounded (rewards bounded). The standard PAC‑Bayes theorem (McAllester, 1999; Catoni, 2007) gives, for any prior P over parameters,

\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\ell_{\text{true}}(\theta)] \le \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\rho}[\ell_{\text{emp}}(\theta)] + \sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{KL}(\rho\|P) + \log(N/\delta)}{2N}}.

Translating back to value yields the first inequality. For the connection to mutual information, the variational identity I(X;R) = \min_{q(r)} \mathbb{E}_x[\mathrm{KL}(p_\theta(r|x)\|q(r))] (Alemi et al., 2016) shows that the information cost upper bounds the expected KL divergence from the encoder to any marginal. Choosing q(r) as the marginal induced by the prior P (or a learned prior) links the regularizer directly to the KL term in the PAC‑Bayes bound. ∎

F.4 Proof of Theorem 18 (Convergence of Gaussian IB)

Theorem 18. The Blahut–Arimoto algorithm for the Gaussian information bottleneck converges to the global optimum of \max I(R;Y) - \lambda I(X;R) when (X,Y) are jointly Gaussian.

Proof. The Gaussian IB problem is a special case of rate‑distortion with a mutual‑information–based distortion measure. The objective is concave in the test channel, and the Blahut–Arimoto alternating minimization procedure is guaranteed to converge monotonically to the unique optimum (Blahut, 1972; Arimoto, 1972). For the Gaussian case, closed‑form updates exist, and convergence is global. ∎

